# =============================================================================
# Python
# =============================================================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# =============================================================================
# Evaluation
# =============================================================================
#
# `evals/datasets/docs/` and `evals/datasets/probes.json` ARE committed. The
# generator is seeded and reproduces them byte-for-byte, but a clone that has to
# run a generator before `pytest` works is a clone where the tests pass against
# whatever the generator produced today, not against the corpus the published
# numbers were measured on. Committing them costs ~180KB and removes that
# ambiguity.
#
# `evals/datasets/manifest.json` is NOT committed: it is derived from the
# documents plus the chunking parameters, so committing it would create a second
# source of truth that silently disagrees with the chunker. The manifest id it
# contains is recorded in `evals/reports/` instead, which is where a *published*
# number belongs.
#
# `runs/` is scratch output from ad-hoc comparisons and ablations.
# =============================================================================
runs/
evals/datasets/manifest.json
evals/datasets/*/manifest.json

# =============================================================================
# Notebooks
# =============================================================================
#
# The committed `.ipynb` files DO carry their outputs. That is the point of the
# `notebooks/` tree: a reader can see the numbers without running anything, and CI
# can diff the rendered result against the source claim.
#
# `.structure_cache/` is NOT committed. It holds mmCIF and PAE files fetched from
# RCSB and AlphaFold DB at run time. They are large, they mirror someone else's
# server, and a PDB entry can be re-deposited -- so a committed copy is a silent
# second source of truth for coordinates. The notebook refetches on demand and
# reuses the cache when it is already there.
#
# `hemoglobin_nglview.html` is generated by `write_html` in the export section of
# the protein notebook: ~6MB of inlined coordinates and widget state.
notebooks/.structure_cache/
notebooks/*.html
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# =============================================================================
# Node
# =============================================================================
#
# The root `.gitignore` was previously a single `node_modules/` line, which is
# why a 150MB tree of dependencies ended up tracked. Ignore it everywhere,
# including nested projects.
# =============================================================================
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# =============================================================================
# Local scratch
# =============================================================================
tmp/
*.log
*.bak
*.orig

# `python -m build` output. A wheel is a build artifact, not source: it is
# reproduced from `pyproject.toml` and CI verifies it, so committing one only
# creates a second thing that can drift from the code it claims to contain.
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
